Non-Tuberculous Mycobacterial (NTM) Lung Disease
Respiratory System
Chronic productive cough, Thin body habitus, Nodular bronchiectasis/NTM cultures

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-030
Reason for Visit: Worsening chronic productive cough, severe lethargy, and low-grade fevers in an elderly, frail patient, requiring urgent assessment for an infective exacerbation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly patient with known underlying bronchiectasis experiencing a significant increase in purulent sputum production, new-onset night sweats, and severe exhaustion preventing basic self-care. Urgent assessment was required to initiate targeted therapy and rule out acute bacterial pneumonia before the regular in-hours clinic opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am constantly exhausted, sweating at night, and I just can't stop bringing up this thick, green phlegm.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been indolently worsening over several months but acutely escalated this week. The patient complains of a chronic, persistent cough predominantly productive of purulent sputum. They report severe, progressive lethargy, low-grade fevers, and night sweats. They have an underlying diagnosis of bronchiectasis. The patient notes unexplained, progressive weight loss over the past year and significant dyspnea on exertion. There is no recent hemoptysis.
Functional Status: Highly fatigued, struggling to maintain nutrition and independent ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, frail, poor nutritional reserve.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 20 breaths/min | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 45 Kg
General: Frail, thin body habitus, predominantly in an older female (consistent with classic Lady Windermere syndrome presentation). Appears chronically fatigued.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals coarse crackles and rhonchi predominantly in the right middle lobe and lingula. No focal bronchial breathing to suggest acute lobar pneumonia. Cardiovascular exam is unremarkable. The diagnostic tetrad of a chronic productive cough, thin body habitus, and underlying nodular bronchiectasis is clinically evident.
Environmental Observations: Neatly kept home, but evidence of untouched meals indicating poor appetite.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with standard bronchiectasis clearance inhalers, but lacking efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Non-Tuberculous Mycobacterial (NTM) Lung Disease - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific localization (middle lobe/lingula vs. apical in TB) and slower, more indolent clinical progression, though formal sputum acid-fast bacilli (AFB) testing is mandatory.

Typical Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high spiking fevers, acute rigors, or focal lobar consolidation signs.

Severe COPD Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of significant smoking history, prominent purulent sputum as the primary feature rather than wheeze, and the classic thin habitus rather than barrel chest.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic phenotype for NTM lung disease (often Mycobacterium avium complex): an elderly, thin female with a chronic productive cough, progressive fatigue, and mid-zone coarse crackles indicative of underlying nodular bronchiectasis. The recent development of night sweats and increased purulent sputum marks an acute exacerbation of this chronic, indolent infection. Waiting until the next in-hours period for assessment would allow the severe fatigue to compromise the patient's nutritional intake and airway clearance further. An urgent after-hours home visit was necessary to perform a thorough lung exam, secure early morning sputum samples for specialized culture, and adjust baseline airway clearance strategies pending specialist review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided three sterile sputum specimen jars. Advised aggressive continuation of chest physiotherapy. Did NOT start standard empirical antibiotics as NTM requires specific, multi-drug regimens guided by culture.
Positive Behavioral Change: Eat a small, protein-dense snack (like Greek yogurt or a boiled egg) exactly every 3 hours rather than trying to eat three large meals; breaking up your nutrition directly prevents the severe fatigue caused by a full stomach pressing on your diaphragm, ensuring you get enough calories to fight the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing for welfare checks and nutritional support.
DME/Safety (Optional): Flutter valve requested to assist with secretion clearance.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 3 days. Patient must deliver the three early morning sputum samples to pathology to confirm NTM cultures.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if the patient coughs up fresh red blood, if they develop a high fever over 39 C, or if breathing becomes severely difficult at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The diagnosis of NTM requires specialized laboratory testing of your sputum to confirm the specific atypical bacteria present.
Clinical Course: If confirmed, the treatment involves a specialized combination of antibiotics taken for many months; standard short-course antibiotics will not cure this infection.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call for help immediately if you feel too exhausted to get out of bed, or if you begin coughing up blood.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your damaged lung tubes have been colonized by a slow-growing environmental bacteria; we need to identify the exact bacteria in the lab first, because using the wrong antibiotics now will make the bacteria resistant and much harder to kill later.
Actionable Steps: You must spit your very first, deepest cough of the morning into the sterile jar on three consecutive days; the bacteria multiply overnight, so this exact morning sample is the only way the lab can accurately identify the infection.

